#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151954 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6c5f369-1196-5ace-9450-815a4ee2cc8f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extended Euclidean Algorithm When the Two Numbers are Coprime"}] :question/id #uuid "df4647c2-73f7-57eb-b013-68d7f170266a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6496" :question/problem "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $36u + 19v = \\text{gcd}(36,19)$. What is the value of $u+v$?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n36 &= \\begin{vmatrix}19 \\cdot 1 & +\\end{vmatrix}17 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 19 &= \\end{vmatrix}17 \\cdot 1\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 2 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 17|= & 2 \\cdot 8\\end{vmatrix}+ \\mid 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(19,36) =1\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, up to (and including) the equation where the greatest common divisor appeared as a remainder, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n17 &= 36 - 19 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n2 &= 19 - 17 \\\\\n1 &= 17 - 2 \\cdot 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 17 - 2 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 17 - (19 - 17) \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 17 \\cdot 9 - 19 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= [36 - 19] \\cdot 9 - 19 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 36 \\cdot 9 - 19 \\cdot 17 \\\\\n&= 36 \\cdot 9 + 19 \\cdot (-17)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=9,v=-17$, and we can write\n\n$$\n1 = 36\\cdot \\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{9} + 19\\cdot (\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{-17})\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nu+v=9+(-17)=-8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151981 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152184 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4caf9fc6-9247-5807-84de-208b731116e7" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extended Euclidean Algorithm When the Two Numbers are Not Coprime"}] :question/id #uuid "ed22bd9f-4dc9-528f-890f-2a81c4d353cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6763" :question/problem "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $30u + 8v = \\text{gcd}(30,8)$. What is the value of $u+v$?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30 &= \\begin{vmatrix}8 \\cdot 3 & +\\end{vmatrix}6 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 8 &= \\end{vmatrix}6 \\cdot 1\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 2 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 6|= & 2 \\cdot 3\\end{vmatrix}+ \\mid 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(30,8) =2\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, up to (and including) the equation where the greatest common divisor appeared as a remainder, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 &= 30 - 8 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n2 &= 8 - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= 8 - 6 \\\\\n&= 8 - (30 - 8 \\cdot 3) \\\\\n&= 8 \\cdot 4 - 30 \\\\\n&= 30 \\cdot (-1) + 8 \\cdot 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=-1$ and $v=4$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nu+v=(-1)+4=3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152163 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6c5f369-1196-5ace-9450-815a4ee2cc8f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extended Euclidean Algorithm When the Two Numbers are Coprime"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d256e3fd-5a91-5a39-98f0-9f55617023f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151020} {:answer/id #uuid "d9fcb2c0-08c1-5713-8524-53538e8c33bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151021} {:answer/id #uuid "1479a963-6dd9-573b-a7f9-387009a90502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151022} {:answer/id #uuid "27bbff50-cd94-5a57-a122-63